            <div xml:id="d791">

                <pb facs="0925" n="781" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg781"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.9331/134: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-01-29T19:00:00+08:00">January 29, 1929—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 29—10:50 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>67. Referring to Department’s 36, January 28, 6 p.m., and Nanking’s January 28,
                    10 p.m., from Perkins. The unwillingness of Dr. Wang to assent formally in
                    writing to your statement of the understanding that the treaty entitles the
                    United States inclusively and unequivocally to nondiscriminatory treatment
                    appears clearly to make reliance upon his general oral assurances impossible.
                    Most urgently do I request of the Department that it reconsider the proposal of
                    accepting a compromise and that it authorize me to inform Dr. Wang that the
                    Senate will begin consideration January 30 of the treaty and that its
                    ratification will be withheld unless assurances along the lines set forth
                    previously are received.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
